\title{A Grid-Based Hahn-Series Model of EML Transseries:\\
Dominance, Asymptotic Comparison, and Ingredients of Real Closure}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-28}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous, fully formalized model of \emph{transseries} --- formal
asymptotic expansions that combine real powers of the variable $x$ with iterated
exponentials and logarithms $\log x, x, e^x, e^{e^x}, \dots$ --- built on top of
the theory of Hahn series. The carrier is the field of Hahn series with real
coefficients over the lexicographically ordered group of \emph{transmonomials},
modeled as the finitely supported maps $\Z \finsupp \R$ from integer \emph{tower
heights} to real exponents. We establish three families of results. First, the
lexicographic order on transmonomials is exactly asymptotic dominance: a higher
tower height dominates any lower one regardless of exponent, and within a fixed
height the larger exponent dominates; in particular $e^x$ dominates $x^a$ for
\emph{every} real $a$, a statement no power-series order can express. Second, the
\emph{asymptotic comparison theorem}: two transseries that agree to all orders are
equal, so a transseries is uniquely determined by its expansion. Third, the
structural \emph{ingredients of real closure}: the value group is divisible, every
one-term transseries admits an $n$-th root and is a square, and we isolate the
precise obstruction (non-divisibility of $\Z$) that prevents Laurent series from
being real closed and that real exponents overcome. We also exhibit the field as a
non-Archimedean ordered field containing $\R$ and construct the exp-shift field
automorphism realizing the self-similarity of the growth-scale tower. All
statements correspond to machine-checked theorems; proof sketches are given
throughout.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
Asymptotic analysis pervades applied mathematics: solutions of differential
equations near singular points, the WKB method, perturbation theory, and the
divergent series of quantum field theory are all routinely manipulated through
their asymptotic expansions. The natural ambient objects for such expansions are
\emph{transseries}: formal series that may involve, simultaneously, real powers of
$x$, logarithms, exponentials, and exponentials of exponentials. Transseries form
the backbone of \'Ecalle's resurgence theory, of the model theory of the real
exponential field, and of the differential algebra of Hardy fields.

A central structural fact is that the field of transseries is an ordered, real
closed, differential field. Our goal is a clean, self-contained, verifiable model
capturing the order-theoretic and field-theoretic core --- enough to state and
prove dominance laws, the asymptotic comparison (uniqueness) theorem, and the
decisive ingredients of real closure --- using as foundation only the general
theory of Hahn series.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A model $\TSeries$ of the transseries field as a Hahn-series field over the
lexicographically ordered transmonomial group $\TransMono = \mathrm{Lex}(\Z
\finsupp \R)$ (\S3), with $\R$ embedded as a subfield.
\item Dominance laws making the lexicographic order coincide with asymptotic
dominance, culminating in \texttt{exp\_dominates\_pow} (\S4), with analytic
counterparts \texttt{isLittleO\_pow\_exp} and \texttt{isLittleO\_expPow\_expExp}.
\item The asymptotic comparison theorem \texttt{agreeToAllOrders\_iff\_eq} (\S5).
\item The ordered-field and non-Archimedean structure (\S6).
\item The ingredients of real closure: \texttt{valueGroup\_divisible},
\texttt{exists\_nthRoot\_term}, \texttt{isSquare\_term}, and the obstruction
\texttt{laurent\_value\_group\_not\_divisible} (\S7).
\item The exp-shift automorphism \texttt{expShiftEquiv} and unboundedness of the
tower \texttt{exists\_exp\_tower\_gt} (\S8).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Preliminaries: Hahn series}
Let $\Gamma$ be a linearly ordered abelian group and $R$ a ring. A \emph{Hahn
series} over $(\Gamma, R)$ is a function $f : \Gamma \to R$ whose support $\{g :
f(g)\neq 0\}$ is well-ordered. Hahn series form a ring under pointwise addition and
convolution; well-orderedness guarantees each product coefficient is a finite sum.

\begin{fact}[Hahn]
If $R$ is a field and $\Gamma$ a linearly ordered abelian group, the Hahn series
over $(\Gamma, R)$ form a field.
\end{fact}

For nonzero $f$, the \emph{order} $\ord(f)$ is the least element of its support. We
package it as a valuation $\orderTop(f) \in \Gamma \cup \{\top\}$ with $\orderTop(0)
= \top$, $\top$ strictly above every $g \in \Gamma$. Key properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item Multiplicativity: $\orderTop(xy) = \orderTop(x) + \orderTop(y)$.
\item Dominant-term addition: if $\orderTop(f) < \orderTop(g)$ then $\orderTop(f+g)
= \orderTop(f)$.
\item Triviality: $\orderTop(f) = \top \iff f = 0$.
\item Single terms: for $c\neq0$, $\mathrm{single}(g,c)$ has $\orderTop = g$ and
leading coefficient $c$.
\end{itemize}

\section{The transmonomial group and the transseries field}

A transseries monomial is a formal product
\[
\cdots (e^{e^x})^{a_2}\,(e^x)^{a_1}\, x^{a_0}\, (\log x)^{a_{-1}} \cdots,
\]
with finitely many nonzero real exponents $a_h$ indexed by the integer \emph{tower
height} $h$: height $0$ is $x$, height $1$ is $e^x$, height $-1$ is $\log x$, height
$2$ is $e^{e^x}$.

\begin{definition}[Transmonomials]
$\TransMono := \mathrm{Lex}(\Z \finsupp \R)$, the finitely supported maps from
heights to real exponents, with the lexicographic order, under pointwise addition.
\end{definition}

The lexicographic order compares at the \emph{smallest} differing index. To make
``highest tower height $=$ most significant'' we store the exponent of height $h$ at
finsupp index $-h$.

\begin{definition}
$\mono(h,a) := \mathrm{toLex}(\mathrm{single}(-h,a))$, the transmonomial with
exponent $a$ at height $h$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Transseries]
$\TSeries := \mathrm{HahnSeries}(\TransMono, \R)$. By Hahn's theorem this is a
field. Write $\term(h,a) := \mathrm{single}(\mono(h,a),1)$, and set $x :=
\term(0,1)$, $e^x := \term(1,1)$, $\log x := \term(-1,1)$. The constant map $C : \R
\to \TSeries$ embeds the reals.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{C\_injective}]
$C : \R \to \TSeries$ is an injective ring homomorphism; $\R$ is a subfield.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$C(r)$ is the one-term series with coefficient $r$ at the identity monomial;
$C(r)=0$ forces $r=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Dominance: the lexicographic order is asymptotic dominance}

\begin{theorem}[Height dominance, \texttt{mono\_lt\_mono\_of\_height}]
For $h < h'$, all $a$, and $a' > 0$, $\mono(h,a) < \mono(h',a')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the finsupp lexicographic criterion, the least differing index is $-h'$. For $d <
-h'$ both vectors vanish; at $-h'$ the left is $0 < a'$. Hence the right side is
larger.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Same-height comparison, \texttt{mono\_lt\_mono\_same}]
For fixed $h$ and $a < a'$, $\mono(h,a) < \mono(h,a')$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exp dominates every power, \texttt{exp\_dominates\_pow}]
For every real $a$, $\mono(0,a) < \mono(1,1)$, i.e.\ $x^a \prec e^x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from height dominance with $h=0<1=h'$, $a'=1>0$. The universally
quantified $a$ is the point: no $\Z$- or $\R$-valued order can place $e^x$ above
$x^a$ for all $a$ at once.
\end{proof}

The one-term valuation $\orderTop(\term(h,a)) = \mono(h,a)$
(\texttt{orderTop\_term}) and multiplicativity (\texttt{orderTop\_mul}) make
$\orderTop$ a faithful measure of asymptotic size.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{isLittleO\_pow\_exp}]
For every $n \in \N$, $x^n = o(e^x)$ as $x \to +\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}[\texttt{isLittleO\_expPow\_expExp}]
For every $n \in \N$, $(e^x)^n = o(e^{e^x})$ as $x \to +\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first is the classical $x^n/e^x \to 0$. The second composes it with $x \mapsto
e^x \to +\infty$. These are the analytic shadows of the formal dominance laws.
\end{proof}

\section{The asymptotic comparison theorem}

\begin{definition}[Agreement to all orders, \texttt{AgreeToAllOrders}]
$a, b$ agree to all orders when for every transmonomial $g$, $g < \orderTop(a-b)$;
that is, $a-b$ is asymptotically smaller than every transmonomial scale.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic comparison, \texttt{agreeToAllOrders\_iff\_eq}]
For all $a,b \in \TSeries$, $\mathrm{AgreeToAllOrders}(a,b) \iff a = b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $\orderTop(a-b) = g \in \TransMono$, instantiating at $g$ gives
$g<g$, absurd; so $\orderTop(a-b)=\top$, whence $a-b=0$. ($\Leftarrow$) $a=b$ gives
$\orderTop(0)=\top$ and $g<\top$ for all $g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[\texttt{agreeToAllOrders\_equivalence}]
Agreement to all orders is an equivalence relation --- indeed it is equality.
\end{corollary}
\begin{corollary}[\texttt{not\_agree\_zero\_of\_ne\_zero}]
A nonzero transseries does not agree to all orders with $0$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The ordered, non-Archimedean field}

\begin{definition}
$\OTSeries := \mathrm{Lex}(\TSeries)$, the field with the leading-term order.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Ordered field, \texttt{orderedField}]
$\OTSeries$ is a field with a compatible linear order (a strict ordered ring).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity, \texttt{term\_pos}]
Every $\term(h,a)$ is positive (leading coefficient $1 > 0$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Non-Archimedean: \texttt{x\_infinitesimal}, \texttt{inv\_x\_infinite}, \texttt{x\_mul\_inv\_x}]
For every $n\in\N$: $(n+1)\cdot x < 1$ and $n < 1/x = \term(0,-1)$; and $x\cdot(1/x)
= 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(n+1)x = \mathrm{single}(\mono(0,1), n+1)$ is dominated by $1$ since $\mono(0,1)>0$;
inverting gives the infinite statement; $\term(0,1)\term(0,-1) = \term(0,0) = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\R$ as ordered subfield, \texttt{C\_lt\_iff}, \texttt{C\_strictMono}]
$C(a) < C(b) \iff a < b$, and $C$ is strictly monotone.
\end{theorem}

\section{Ingredients of real closure}

\begin{theorem}[Divisible value group, \texttt{valueGroup\_divisible}]
For every $g$ and $n>0$ there is $g'$ with $n\cdot g' = g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $g' = \mathrm{toLex}(\tfrac1n \cdot \mathrm{ofLex}(g))$: divide every real
exponent by $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monomial roots, \texttt{exists\_nthRoot\_term}]
For every $h,a$ and $n>0$ there is $y$ with $y^n = \term(h,a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$y = \term(h, a/n)$; then $y^n = \term(h, n(a/n)) = \term(h,a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Squares, \texttt{isSquare\_term}]
Every $\term(h,a)$ is a square: $\term(h,a) = \term(h,a/2)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Obstruction, \texttt{laurent\_value\_group\_not\_divisible}]
There is no integer $k$ with $2k = 1$; the Laurent value group $\Z$ is not
divisible.
\end{theorem}

\noindent\textbf{Discussion.} Laurent series use integer exponents; halving an
odd-exponent monomial leaves $\Z$, so $x$ has no square root and the field is not
real closed. Transseries use real exponents, restoring divisibility and hence
monomial root extraction. The full real-closure theorem additionally extends root
extraction from monomials to general series via Newton polygons over the real
closed residue field $\R$; the present development formalizes the value-group
ingredient and the monomial-level roots.

\section{The exp-shift automorphism}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{expShift}, \texttt{expShiftEquiv}]
The exp-shift is the ring homomorphism induced by $h \mapsto h+1$ on
transmonomials; with the inverse log-shift ($h \mapsto h-1$) it forms a field
isomorphism $\mathtt{expShiftEquiv} : \TSeries \xrightarrow{\cong} \TSeries$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Action on generators, \texttt{expShift\_var}, \texttt{expShift\_log}]
$\mathtt{expShift}(\term(h,a)) = \term(h+1,a)$; in particular $x \mapsto e^x$, $e^x
\mapsto e^{e^x}$, $\log x \mapsto x$, and real constants are fixed.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Inverse laws, \texttt{expShift\_logShift}, \texttt{logShift\_expShift}]
The log-shift is a two-sided inverse, so \texttt{expShiftEquiv} is an automorphism.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded tower, \texttt{exists\_exp\_tower\_gt}]
For every $g$ there is $n \in \N$ with $g < \mono(n,1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$g$ has finite support with maximal active height $h_{\max}$; any $n > h_{\max}$
dominates by height dominance.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}
\paragraph{Dominance comparison.} Scan heights highest-to-lowest, returning at the
first disagreement; $O(k\log k)$ for $k$ active heights.
\paragraph{Leading-term extraction.} Take the maximum transmonomial; its
coefficient is the leading coefficient; $O(mk)$ for $m$ terms.
\paragraph{Monomial $n$-th root.} Divide every exponent by $n$; constant time per
height. With integer exponents this fails exactly when $n \nmid a$.

\section{Applications and discussion}
The model is the algebraic setting for formal solutions of nonlinear ODEs near
irregular singular points (justified by the comparison theorem), for resurgence and
Borel--\'Ecalle summation, and for the model theory of $(\R,\exp)$. Encoding tower
height $h$ at index $-h$ aligns asymptotic dominance with the ambient lexicographic
order; using real exponents is what makes the value group divisible, which the
obstruction theorem shows is necessary for real closure.

\section{Future work}
Differential structure (a derivation extending $d/dx$); full real closure via
Newton polygons; composition and genuine $\exp/\log$ operators; summability and the
Borel transform; and a certified reconstruction pipeline from leading-term
sequences back to transseries.

